<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suebi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Suevi, Suavi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Suebi were a large group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, first reported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, who originated near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> River in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Suebian groups spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>; a powerful alliance led by the Marcomanni was crushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> of the late 2nd century, and the Alemanni later took Roman territory in what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. Around 406 many Suebi moved west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>, and by 409 a large group entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, founding the Kingdom of the Suebi in Gallaecia, which lasted until 585 when it was absorbed by the Visigothic Kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Archaeologically, the forerunners of the Suebi before contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> are associated with the Jastorf culture, which lay in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. In his report of events in 58 BCE, Caesar described the Suebi as a single large nomadic tribe living between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Elbe. In the same year he confronted a large army from beyond the Rhine led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus">Ariovistus</a>, who had a Suebian wife and may have been Suebian himself. They spoke a West Germanic language, believed to be the main predecessor of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A powerful Suebian alliance led by the Marcomanni settled north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and kept a tense relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Marcomannic wars, fought mainly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, ended in a crushing defeat that destroyed older power structures on the frontier, and many Suebi moved into the empire. The diverse group who came to be called the Alemanni took Roman territory in what later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> in 378 the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Alans unsettled the Middle Danube. Around 406 many Suebi moved west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>, and a large group entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> by 409 and divided it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans by agreement with the rebel general Gerontius. The Suebi were the last of the three to hold an independent kingdom, in Gallaecia, which endured until 585, when the Visigothic kingdom absorbed it; it had been a Visigothic vassal from 456 or 457.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, a cultural region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, still bears a version of the Suebian name, and the Kingdom of the Suebi left its legacy in local place-names. Many Danubian Suebi were assimilated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Langobards</a>, who entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after 568. Some northern Suebi appear to have survived near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the name appears in Norse sources, where Sváfa, whose name means Suebian, is a Valkyrie.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suebi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the last day of 406, according to the traditional date, Alans crossed the Rhine with the Vandals and <b>Suebi</b>, entered Gaul and in 409 Hispania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Vandals, Alans and <b>Suebi</b> broke into the peninsula in 409, they settled in the west and south, and the Roman government held the north-east as its base, using Visigothic allies to fight the invaders in 416–418.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Volga by 370 pushed Goths and Alans westward and sent thousands of Goths over the Danube in 376, and by 430 they had built a vast if short-lived empire on Rome's Danubian frontier under whose hegemony Goths, Gepids, Sarmatians, Heruli, <b>Suebi</b>, Sciri and Rugii kept their own kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's roots lay in the 5th century, when the <b>Suebi</b> settled in the Roman province of Gallaecia, made Bracara Augusta (Braga) their capital and founded a kingdom known first as the Kingdom of the Suebi and later as the Kingdom of Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Vandals, Alans and <b>Suebi</b> entered Hispania in 409, the province had no army capable of resisting them and depended on Roman forces and allies sent from outside.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They first appear among the confederates of Ariovistus whom Julius Caesar defeated in Gaul in 58 BCE, drawn up by tribe beside the Harudes, Tribocci, Vangiones, Nemetes, Sedusii and <b>Suebi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
